{
  "assessment": "The source provides a quantitative Kramers-Langer rate formula for the identical-ion cluster with a tunable aspect ratio alpha, and a qualitative suppression for the isotope-substituted cluster. The identical-ion case yields a numeric control (alpha) and a numeric readout (inversion rate) with a fitted temperature and background rate. The isotope-substituted case lacks a quantitative rate formula over a shared alpha domain, so the hinted two-formula cluster is not fully supported. The extraction focuses on the identical-ion K-L rate and the isotope-substituted barrier as a fixed condition, setting case_authoring_ready true because the identical-ion case supports a numeric control, readout, mechanism, and response.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Akhil Ayyadevara, Symmetry-Controlled Thermal Activation in Pyramidal Coulomb Clusters: Testing Kramers-Langer Theory, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/xsdn-srjd",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/xsdn-srjd",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2601.04883v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper studies thermally activated inversion dynamics of a five-ion square-pyramidal Coulomb cluster in a Paul trap, testing multidimensional Kramers-Langer theory. It reports experimental inversion rates for identical 40Ca+ ions as a function of trap aspect ratio alpha, and a qualitative suppression for isotope-substituted 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 clusters. The main quantitative cluster is the identical-ion K-L rate formula with fitted temperature and background rate.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "kramers_langer_rate",
      "statement": "The escape rate from a local minimum predicted by multidimensional Kramers-Langer theory is kappa = N * (lambda^+ / 2pi) * sqrt(det U / |det U'|) * exp(-E_b / k_B T), where E_b is the energy barrier, U and U' are Hessians at equilibrium and saddle, lambda^+ is the single positive eigenvalue of the dynamical matrix at the saddle, and N is the number of equivalent pathways.",
      "equations": [
        "kappa = N * (lambda^+ / (2*pi)) * sqrt(det(U) / |det(U')|) * exp(-E_b / (k_B * T))",
        "det(m * lambda^2 * I + gamma * lambda * I + U') = 0"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["kappa", "N", "lambda^+", "U", "U'", "E_b", "k_B", "T", "gamma", "m"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "N_pathways_identical",
          "value": 4,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "multiplicity factor for identical-ion cluster",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 147,
              "line_end": 147,
              "support": "We also set N = 4 since the apex ion can replace any of the four base ions."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 137,
          "line_end": 145,
          "support": "The escape rate from a local minimum predicted by the multidimensional K-L theory is kappa = N * (lambda^+ / 2pi) * sqrt(det U / |det U'|) * exp(-E_b / k_B T), with definitions of E_b, U, U', lambda^+, and N."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "pseudo_rotation_pathway",
      "statement": "For identical ions, the inversion proceeds via a Berry pseudo-rotation mechanism where the apex ion replaces a base ion and that base ion moves to the new apex, enabled by permutation symmetry. The activation barrier is approximately two orders of magnitude lower than the umbrella inversion, and lowering alpha reduces the barrier.",
      "equations": [
        "E_b(pseudo-rotation) ~ 20 mK, approximately two orders of magnitude lower than umbrella inversion (~1 K)"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["alpha", "E_b"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 96,
          "line_end": 98,
          "support": "The cluster finds a significantly lower-energy pathway, whereby the apex ion replaces a base ion, while that base ion moves to the new apex. This cooperative exchange is analogous to the pseudo-rotation mechanism. Along this pathway, the ions maintain larger pairwise separations compared to the umbrella inversion, greatly reducing the Coulomb energy cost and lowering the activation barrier by approximately two orders of magnitude. In addition, lowering the trap aspect ratio alpha reduces the activation barrier."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 448,
          "line_end": 448,
          "support": "This path relaxes to the Berry pseudo-rotation MEP, avoiding the high-energy center and reducing the activation barrier by two orders of magnitude (from ~1 K to ~20 mK)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "turnstile_rotation_pathway",
      "statement": "For the isotope-substituted cluster (44Ca+ at apex), the pseudo-rotation is not viable; the inversion proceeds via a turnstile rotation with a much higher activation barrier of approximately 270 mK, which suppresses thermal activation at experimental temperatures.",
      "equations": [
        "E_b(turnstile) ~ 270 mK"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["E_b"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "E_b_turnstile",
          "value": 0.27,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "K",
          "role": "activation barrier for turnstile rotation in isotope-substituted cluster",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 450,
              "line_end": 450,
              "support": "The activation barrier is ~270 mK, which is significantly lower than the umbrella inversion but sufficiently high to suppress thermal activation at experimental temperatures."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 109,
          "line_end": 109,
          "support": "The pseudo rotation is no longer viable as it does not invert the pyramid. Instead, the ions have to undergo a cooperative exchange of three ions akin to a turnstile rotation, with much higher energy barriers."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 450,
          "line_end": 450,
          "support": "The cNEB optimization converges to a turnstile rotation mechanism. The activation barrier is ~270 mK."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "identical_ions_40Ca",
      "statement": "Cluster of five identical 40Ca+ ions.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 79,
          "line_end": 79,
          "support": "We experimentally realize the inversion dynamics of a square-pyramidal cluster of five 40Ca+ ions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "isotope_substituted_44Ca_apex",
      "statement": "Cluster of 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 with the heavier isotope at the apex.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 109,
          "line_end": 109,
          "support": "We break the permutation symmetry by substituting one ion with a heavier isotope (44Ca+), which preferentially occupies the apex of the pyramid."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "alpha_approx_1_09",
      "statement": "Trap aspect ratio alpha approximately 1.09.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ~ 1.09), the (40Ca+)5 cluster undergoes rapid inversions, whereas the 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 cluster remains locked."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "photon_scattering_rate_22MHz",
      "statement": "Photon scattering rate Gamma/2pi ~ 22 MHz.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 126,
          "line_end": 126,
          "support": "The photon scattering rate (Gamma/2pi ~ 22 MHz) is much larger than the secular frequencies."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "secular_frequencies_1MHz",
      "statement": "Secular frequencies omega_x,z / 2pi ~ 1 MHz.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 126,
          "line_end": 126,
          "support": "The photon scattering rate (Gamma/2pi ~ 22 MHz) is much larger than the secular frequencies (omega_x,z/2pi ~ 1 MHz)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "rf_drive_18_26MHz",
      "statement": "RF drive frequency Omega_rf = 2pi * 18.26 MHz.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 384,
          "line_end": 384,
          "support": "We confine the calcium ions in an end-cap type 3D Paul trap with an rf-drive frequency Omega_rf = 2pi * 18.26 MHz."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "cooling_beam_red_detuned_3Gamma",
      "statement": "Cooling beam red detuned by ~3 Gamma.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 412,
          "line_end": 412,
          "support": "The cooling beam is red detuned by ~3 Gamma."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "friction_gamma_over_m_6e3",
      "statement": "Friction coefficient gamma/m ~ 6e3 s^-1 in MD simulations.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 497,
          "line_end": 497,
          "support": "The friction is set to the experimental cooling value (gamma/m ~ 6e3 s^-1)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "name": "alpha",
      "definition": "Trap aspect ratio alpha = omega_z / omega_x, controlled by dc voltage.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "domain_kind": "continuous_interval",
      "minimum": 1.05,
      "maximum": 1.29,
      "allowed_values": [],
      "domain_statement": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima of V are two sets of inversion-broken square pyramids.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 90,
          "line_end": 90,
          "support": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima of V are two sets of inversion-broken square pyramids."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "readouts": [
    {
      "name": "inversion_rate_kappa",
      "definition": "Inversion rate kappa extracted from exponential distribution of dwell times between sign flips of the parity-odd octupole moment psi_30.",
      "unit": "s^-1",
      "statistic": "Exponential fit to dwell time histogram tail; MD rates from more than 2000 trajectories.",
      "uncertainty": "Experimental rates have uncertainty from exponential fits; fitted temperature T = 1.8 +/- 0.1 mK; background rate R_bg = 0.10 +/- 0.03 s^-1.",
      "resolution": null,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 133,
          "line_end": 133,
          "support": "We extract the inversion rate kappa from an exponential distribution of the dwell time tau from more than 2000 trajectories."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 412,
          "line_end": 412,
          "support": "From the psi_30 time series, we bin the dwell times into a histogram and fit an exponential to the tail."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "psi_30",
      "definition": "Parity-odd octupole moment psi_30 = integral d^3 r rho_e(r) Y_3,0(r), used to distinguish inversion polarity.",
      "unit": "arbitrary",
      "statistic": "Time series from fluorescence images; sign flips indicate inversions.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified.",
      "resolution": null,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 90,
          "line_end": 90,
          "support": "The inversion breaking is clearly distinguished by the sign of the parity-odd octupole moment, psi_30 = integral d^3 r rho_e(r) Y_3,0(r)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "kl_rate_identical_ions",
      "statement": "For the identical-ion cluster, the experimentally measured inversion rate follows the Kramers-Langer prediction over two orders of magnitude for alpha < alpha_th ~ 1.1, with a fitted temperature T = 1.8 +/- 0.1 mK. For alpha > alpha_th, the rate saturates to R_bg = 0.10 +/- 0.03 s^-1 due to background-gas collisions.",
      "quantitative_form": "kappa = N * (lambda^+ / 2pi) * sqrt(det U / |det U'|) * exp(-E_b / k_B T), with N=4; for alpha < alpha_th ~ 1.1, kappa follows this prediction with T = 1.8 +/- 0.1 mK; for alpha > alpha_th, kappa = R_bg = 0.10 +/- 0.03 s^-1.",
      "mechanism_ids": ["kramers_langer_rate", "pseudo_rotation_pathway"],
      "control_names": ["alpha"],
      "control_domains": [
        {
          "control_name": "alpha",
          "domain_kind": "continuous_interval",
          "minimum": 1.05,
          "maximum": 1.29,
          "allowed_values": [],
          "domain_statement": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima are square pyramids; the K-L fit applies for alpha < alpha_th ~ 1.1.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 90,
              "line_end": 90,
              "support": "For 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, the global minima of V are two sets of inversion-broken square pyramids."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 151,
              "line_end": 151,
              "support": "For alpha < alpha_th ~ 1.1, it grows rapidly as the barrier height is lowered, whereas for alpha > alpha_th, it saturates to R_bg."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "readout_names": ["inversion_rate_kappa"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["identical_ions_40Ca", "photon_scattering_rate_22MHz", "secular_frequencies_1MHz"],
      "applicability": "Valid for the identical-ion (40Ca+)5 cluster in the bistable pyramid regime 1.05 < alpha < 1.29, with Doppler cooling at ~22 MHz scattering rate and secular frequencies ~1 MHz. The K-L prediction applies for alpha < alpha_th ~ 1.1; for alpha > alpha_th, the rate is dominated by background collisions.",
      "statistical_basis": "Experimental rates from exponential fits to dwell time histograms; MD rates from more than 2000 trajectories; fitted temperature and background rate from least-squares fit to K-L theory.",
      "uncertainty": "Fitted temperature T = 1.8 +/- 0.1 mK; background rate R_bg = 0.10 +/- 0.03 s^-1; uncertainty convention not specified.",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 151,
          "line_end": 151,
          "support": "The experimentally measured inversion rate exhibits a clear threshold behavior. For alpha < alpha_th ~ 1.1, it grows rapidly as the barrier height is lowered, whereas for alpha > alpha_th, it saturates to R_bg = 0.10 +/- 0.03 s^-1 due to background-gas collisions. The excess rate for alpha < alpha_th follows the K-L prediction over two orders of magnitude for a fitted temperature of T = 1.8 +/- 0.1 mK."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "isotope_substitution_suppression",
      "statement": "Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ~ 1.09), the isotope-substituted cluster 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 remains locked in one orientation over long periods, showing suppression of thermally activated inversions compared to the identical-ion cluster, which undergoes rapid inversions. The activation barrier for the turnstile rotation is ~270 mK.",
      "quantitative_form": "Qualitative: inversion rate for isotope-substituted cluster is much lower than for identical-ion cluster at alpha ~ 1.09; barrier E_b ~ 270 mK for turnstile rotation.",
      "mechanism_ids": ["turnstile_rotation_pathway"],
      "control_names": ["alpha"],
      "control_domains": [
        {
          "control_name": "alpha",
          "domain_kind": "fixed_value",
          "minimum": 1.09,
          "maximum": 1.09,
          "allowed_values": [1.09],
          "domain_statement": "alpha ~ 1.09 for the comparison.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 111,
              "line_end": 111,
              "support": "Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ~ 1.09), the (40Ca+)5 cluster undergoes rapid inversions, whereas the 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 cluster remains locked."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "readout_names": ["inversion_rate_kappa"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["isotope_substituted_44Ca_apex", "alpha_approx_1_09"],
      "applicability": "Valid for the isotope-substituted cluster 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 at alpha ~ 1.09; the suppression is qualitative, no quantitative rate formula is provided.",
      "statistical_basis": "Qualitative observation from fluorescence time traces; no rate extraction reported for the isotope-substituted cluster.",
      "uncertainty": "Not specified.",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "Under identical trapping conditions (alpha ~ 1.09), the (40Ca+)5 cluster undergoes rapid inversions [Fig. 1(c)], whereas the 44Ca+(40Ca+)4 cluster remains locked in one orientation over long periods [Fig. 1(d)]."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 450,
          "line_end": 450,
          "support": "The activation barrier is ~270 mK, which is significantly lower than the umbrella inversion but sufficiently high to suppress thermal activation at experimental temperatures."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_ambiguities": [
    {
      "ambiguity_id": "alpha_th_approx",
